    <table>
      <thead><tr><th>Situation</th><th>Backup</th><th>Result</th></tr></thead>
      <tbody>
        <tr><td>New file, migrations folder given</td><td>No (the file did not exist before opening)</td><td>All migrations applied in one transaction</td></tr>
        <tr><td>Existing file, journal up to date</td><td>No</td><td>Opened as is; <code>needed</code> is false</td></tr>
        <tr><td>Existing file, pending migrations</td><td>Yes, before any change</td><td>Pending migrations applied; journal rows inserted with <code>applied_at</code></td></tr>
        <tr><td>Existing file, journal without <code>name</code> column</td><td>Yes</td><td>Columns <code>name</code> and <code>applied_at</code> added, rows backfilled, then pending migrations applied</td></tr>
        <tr><td>Legacy row matches no local migration</td><td>No backup is taken: planning fails first</td><td><code>DatabaseError</code> with operation <code>migrate</code>: "Legacy migration journal does not match local migrations"</td></tr>
        <tr><td><code>:memory:</code></td><td>Never</td><td>Migrations applied if a folder is given</td></tr>
        <tr><td>No migrations folder</td><td>Never</td><td>Opened without creating tables</td></tr>
        </tbody>
    </table>
    <p>Backfill matching works like this: a legacy row's <code>created_at</code> is truncated to whole seconds and compared with each migration's folder timestamp. If exactly one migration matches, it is used. Otherwise the hash breaks the tie, and as a last resort any migration with the same hash is used.</p>
  </section>

  <section id="backup-files" class="doc-section">
    <h2>Backup file anatomy</h2>
    <DocCode language="text" title="Pre-migration backup name">
{"backups/cadcamfun-20261015-034512.123-6f1c2d4e-8a90-4b7c-9e21-3f5a6b7c8d9e-pre-migrate.db\n        \u2514 prefix \u2518 \u2514 UTC timestamp \u2518 \u2514 random UUID                      \u2518"}
    </DocCode>
    <ul>
      <li>The prefix is the database file name without <code>.db</code>, followed by a dash.</li>
      <li>The timestamp is UTC with millisecond precision. If the newest existing backup has the same or a later timestamp, the new one is set 1 ms after it, so lexical order always equals creation order.</li>
      <li>The snapshot is written to <code>&lt;name&gt;.partial</code> with <code>VACUUM INTO</code>, then renamed. A crash leaves at most a <code>.partial</code> file, which is removed in a <code>finally</code> block.</li>
      <li>After a successful backup, only the five newest files matching the exact pattern are kept. Files you copy into the directory under other names are never deleted.</li>
      <li><code>VACUUM INTO</code> produces a compact, self-contained database with no WAL sibling, so a backup can be opened directly.</li>
    </ul>
    <DocCode language="bash" title="Restore a pre-migration backup (application stopped)">
{"DATA=\u0022/absolute/path/to/data\u0022\ncp \u0022$DATA/cadcamfun.db\u0022 \u0022$DATA/cadcamfun.db.broken\u0022         # keep the failed state\nrm -f \u0022$DATA/cadcamfun.db-wal\u0022 \u0022$DATA/cadcamfun.db-shm\u0022\ncp \u0022$DATA/backups/cadcamfun-<timestamp>-<uuid>-pre-migrate.db\u0022 \u0022$DATA/cadcamfun.db\u0022\nsqlite3 -readonly \u0022$DATA/cadcamfun.db\u0022 'PRAGMA quick_check;'   # expect: ok"}
    </DocCode>
    <DocCallout type="warn" title="Restoring rolls back schema and data">
      <p>A restored backup is the state just before the upgrade. Edits made after that upgrade are not in it, and the next launch will try the same migration again. Fix the cause of the failure before restarting.</p>
    </DocCallout>
  </section>

