  <section id="write-patterns" class="doc-section">
    <h2>Writing safely</h2>
    <ul>
      <li>Wrap multi-statement changes in a Drizzle transaction inside one <code>query</code> call: <code>database.query("op", (db) =&gt; db.transaction((tx) =&gt; ...))</code>. Separate <code>query</code> calls are separate transactions.</li>
      <li>Give each query a stable, dotted operation name (<code>projects.list</code>, <code>toolpaths.update</code>). It is the only context a <code>DatabaseError</code> carries besides the cause, and it appears in logs.</li>
      <li>Generate ids with <code>newId()</code> (a UUID) or let the column default do it; do not build ids from names.</li>
      <li>Validate JSON columns when reading them. The declared TypeScript type is not checked by SQLite.</li>
      <li>Use <code>LocalKeyValue</code> through the sidecar key-value service rather than writing rows directly, so the key and value validation is applied.</li>
    </ul>
    <DocCode language="ts" title="A transactional write">
{"import { Database, schema } from \u0022@cadcamfun/db\u0022\nimport { eq } from \u0022drizzle-orm\u0022\nimport { Effect } from \u0022effect\u0022\n\nconst renameProject = (id: string, name: string) => Effect.gen(function* () {\n  const database = yield* Database\n  return yield* database.query(\u0022projects.rename\u0022, (db) =>\n    db.transaction((tx) => {\n      const updated = tx.update(schema.projectTable)\n        .set({ name, updatedAt: new Date() })\n        .where(eq(schema.projectTable.id, id))\n        .returning().all()\n      if (updated.length === 0) throw new Error(`project ${id} not found`)\n      return updated[0]\n    }))\n})"}
    </DocCode>
    <p>To create rows with explicit ids, use <code>schema.newId()</code>; <code>@cadcamfun/db</code> re-exports the schema module as the <code>schema</code> namespace, not as top-level names.</p>
  </section>

